1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,571
How much does the bank earn on each payment, and who decides it?

2
00:00:03,571 --> 00:00:07,940
Fees can't be hard-coded, because every merchant negotiates its own.

3
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:14,845
So the commission module keeps fee rules, each for a website, a merchant, a card scheme, or the whole bank.

4
00:00:14,845 --> 00:00:23,107
When several rules match, the most specific one wins: a website beats a merchant, which beats a card scheme, which beats the bank.

5
00:00:23,107 --> 00:00:30,273
The fee is a percent, rounded once to whole tiyin, plus a fixed part, kept between a minimum and a maximum.

6
00:00:30,273 --> 00:00:32,952
A fee that would take the whole amount is refused.

7
00:00:32,952 --> 00:00:39,654
Rules are never edited: a change adds a rule or ends one, and a second admin must approve it.

8
00:00:39,654 --> 00:00:46,023
Two active rules for the same scope can never apply at the same moment, and the database itself refuses them.

9
00:00:46,023 --> 00:00:51,583
To change a fee, the old rule ends at a chosen moment and the new one starts there.

10
00:00:51,583 --> 00:00:58,416
Other modules ask one question, what is the fee for this payment, and the payment keeps the rule and the amount.

11
00:00:58,416 --> 00:01:03,952
The module has its own schema and database user, built by a new shared persistence library.

12
00:01:03,952 --> 00:01:10,297
Unit tests cover the formula and the scope order, and integration tests run on a real Postgres.

13
00:01:10,297 --> 00:01:15,702
Under load, more than thirty thousand fee quotes came back in under three milliseconds.

14
00:01:15,702 --> 00:01:18,535
Next, the terminal each payment goes through.

